window.docContents[104].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Interior-and-Boundary-Regularity":{"contents":"\n\nElliptic regularity reconnects the variational and classical theories: a weak\nsolution produced by Lax–Milgram gains as many Sobolev derivatives as the\ncoefficients and data permit.\n\nDifference quotients are defined on  functions, even when pointwise\nderivatives are unavailable, and their product identities separate the\nsolution, coefficients, and cutoffs. They regularize within the energy space,\nwith no mollification. Cutoff functions introduce commutator terms, boundary\ncharts introduce Jacobian factors, and\nlower-order coefficients introduce additional forcing.\n\nEvans (2010, Sections 6.3.1–6.3.2, pp. 327–343) proves interior \nregularity for arbitrary  weak solutions, global boundary regularity\nfor homogeneous Dirichlet solutions, higher-order Sobolev regularity, and\nsmoothness under smooth data. We prove Evans's compact-set interior theorem\nfor arbitrary  weak solutions, and also a zero-trace version localized\nby an atlas cutoff, which is the form the global Dirichlet assembly uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Elliptic Regularity and Solution Operators","header":"5.6.1. Interior and Boundary Regularity","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Elliptic-Regularity-and-Solution-Operators--Interior-and-Boundary-Regularity"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Differentiation___-Gradients___-and-Laplacians":{"contents":"Consider the scalar function . To differentiate only in\nthe first coordinate, write:\n\n-- Differentiate in x; y remains a parameter.\n#cas [derive x] (sin (x * y))\n\n\nThe compact report says:\n\n#cas [derive x]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (sin (x * y))\nOutput (scalar ℝ): cos(x*y)*y\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe domain line identifies the ordered real coordinate space inferred from\nthe free variables. The input and output lines identify scalar-valued\nexpressions; the output is the partial derivative , not the\nfull derivative of a one-variable function. With set_option cas.verbose true,\nMore details also shows the sine chain rule, the product rule for , the raw expression\n, and the checked normalization to\n.\n\nFor the full gradient, name its coordinates and their order:\n\n-- The written variable order fixes the\n-- output coordinate order.\n#cas [grad x, y] (sin (x * y))\n\n\nThe result is\n\n#cas [grad x, y]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (sin (x * y))\nOutput (vector ℝ^2; order x, y): (cos(x*y)*y, cos(x*y)*x)\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThus grad x, y means\n. Writing grad y, x requests the same two\npartials in the opposite output order. This explicit convention avoids\nguessing whether a multivariable derive request meant one coordinate or an\nentire gradient.\n\nThe Laplacian computes each diagonal second partial and sums the results:\n\n-- Sum the diagonal second derivatives in x and y.\n#cas [laplacian x, y] (x ^ 2 + y ^ 2)\n\n\n#cas [laplacian x, y]\nDomain: (x, y) ∈ ℝ^2\nInput (scalar ℝ): (x ^ 2 + y ^ 2)\nOutput (scalar ℝ): 4\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe final bridge theorem identifies the assembled reflected expression with\nMathlib's analytic gradient or the analytic Laplacian defined in the\nfoundations chapter.\n\nA real power is differentiated by the rule behind Mathlib's\nHasDerivAt.rpow, and the report records the positivity of the base:\n\n-- A real exponent; the base must be positive.\n#cas [derive x] (x ^ (3 / 2 : ℝ))\n\n\n#cas [derive x]\nDomain: x ∈ ℝ\nInput (scalar ℝ): (x ^ (3 / 2 : ℝ))\nOutput (scalar ℝ): 3/2*sqrt(x)\nConditions: x > 0\n\n[CAS.Command.details] More details\n\n\nThe raw derivative is ; the normal form reads the\nliteral exponent  back as a square root. A symbolic exponent stays a\nreal power, so #cas [derive x] (x ^ y) reports  under the\nsame condition.\n\nThe theorem tactic can also differentiate functions outside the calculator's real-expression\ngrammar. Mathlib supplies the arithmetic and chain rules; derivative families in the local\ncontext supply the function-specific step:\n\nexample (f f' : ℝ → ℝ) (hf : ∀ x, HasDerivAt f (f' x) x) (x : ℝ) :\n    deriv (fun t ↦ f (t ^ 2) + f t) x = f' (x ^ 2) * (2 * x) + f' x := by\n  cas\n\n\nA theorem tagged @[cas_deriv] makes its derivative rule available to subsequent calls.\nThe rule's premises still have to be proved. This route also handles complex scalar calculus:\n\nexample (z : ℂ) :\n    HasDerivAt (fun w : ℂ ↦ Complex.exp (w ^ 2)) (2 * z * Complex.exp (z ^ 2)) z := by\n  cas\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Using Verified Computation","header":"10.5.3. Differentiation, Gradients, and Laplacians","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Using-Verified-Computation--Differentiation___-Gradients___-and-Laplacians"}});